//! lotus/stereo_collapse_silence.rye — ALES152: the interior silence-capper carried into stereo, ONE JOINT SILENCE capped in ONE LOCKSTEP — the stripper of ALES148
//! kept its pauses. ALES100 named `collapse_silence`: segment a mono clip, then cap each `.silent` run to a maximum, keeping the pauses yet shortening the long ones —
//! "truncate silence to a quarter-second," the single most-reached-for gesture in every voice-memo cleanup, because it shortens dead air without deleting the breath
//! that makes speech legible. That is a mono edit. Carried into stereo it meets the wall ALES148 met and no earlier: a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips
//! whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length, and ALES147 proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY — their silences differ in count and
//! boundary. Capping each channel by its OWN silence would cap different runs to different lengths and desync the pair, tearing the stereo image apart. So this rung
//! cannot run the mono capper twice; it must decide ONE cap that serves BOTH.
//!
//! The rule, stated once — the JOINT-SILENCE CAP: read the jointly-silent frames through ALES148's own silence intersection (a frame is dead air only where NEITHER
//! speaker carries sound), coalesce consecutive jointly-silent frames into jointly-silent RUNS, and for each run longer than `max_silence` cut only the EXCESS (keep the
//! run's first `min(count, max_silence)` samples, cut the rest) from BOTH channels in lockstep, walking back to front. A frame silent in only one channel is KEPT whole —
//! its other speaker carries sound; only the shared dead air is shortened, and only its excess is cut, so the breath stays. Both channels are cut at identical positions,
//! so they leave exactly as long as each other: the stereo image stays whole.
//!
//! The crux — two laws, each tying this rung to a proven predecessor so it can never drift. THE ZERO-CAP LAW: stereo_collapse_silence(max=0) equals ALES148's
//! stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on BOTH channels — capping every joint-silent run to zero removes all joint silence, exactly the stereo stripper. THE CAP LAW: the
//! result equals the hand-built keep where each jointly-silent run keeps its first min(count, max_silence) samples and every other frame stays whole; a cap at least as
//! large as the longest joint run leaves the master unchanged; monotone in max_silence — a larger cap keeps a master at least as long. It invents no new measurement and
//! no new sample: it reads its silences through ALES148/ALES147 and removes whole excess-silent frames through ALES2's own `cut`, fabricating nothing and reordering
//! nothing. What is NEW is the composition rule — capping the intersection of two independent silences — and its honesty is that running the proven mono collapse per
//! channel would DESYNC the pair, where this caps once for both and keeps them balanced.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES152.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-055202_fill-ales152-lotus-stereo-collapse-silence.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148) — the stereo silence stripper, the zero-cap twin, imported by the selftest to prove the zero-cap law; its silence
//! intersection is the joint-silence this rung caps; lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147) — the stereo voice-activity segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences
//! over one shared frame grid, its `segment_stereo`, `StereoSegments`, `StereoSegmentError` reused whole; lotus/segment.rye (ALES95) — the mono segmenter (the frame grid
//! and `.silent` verdict, through ALES147) and `max_segments`; lotus/collapse_silence.rye (ALES100) — the mono interior capper, imported by the selftest to prove the
//! lockstep law (that running it per channel would desync the pair); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the
//! Clip, its `cut`, its `max_clip`, and the EditError reused whole; lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94) — the `.silent` verdict this intersects.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. This rung WRITES — it edits both clips —
//! yet only by removing whole excess-silent frames from within each jointly-silent run through ALES2's own cut, on both channels in lockstep; it fabricates no samples,
//! reorders nothing, and never touches a non-silent sample or the kept head of any joint pause. NOT a transcript, not a real adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no
//! network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_collapse_silence.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const segment = @import("segment.rye");
const stereo_segment = @import("stereo_segment.rye");
const stereo_trim_silence = @import("stereo_trim_silence.rye");
const collapse_silence = @import("collapse_silence.rye");

pub const stereo_collapse_silence_version = "20260815.055202";

/// The most frames a span can carry — one per sample, when `frame_len` is 1 — bounded by the clip's own sample ceiling. Names the jointly-silent mask's size once.
const max_frames: u32 = timeline.max_clip;

/// The stereo capper's faults are the stereo segmenter's OWN faults (ALES147's StereoSegmentError, itself ALES95's SegmentError: the classifier's band/floor faults, plus
/// BadFrame and the honest unreachable SegmentsFull) and the editor's OWN faults (ALES2's EditError from cut), each reused by name. The stereo lift invents none of its
/// own, and `max_silence` is a plain count with no illegal value (a huge cap keeps every joint pause whole); an empty clip refuses BadFrame through the segmenter.
pub const StereoCollapseError = stereo_segment.StereoSegmentError || timeline.EditError;

/// Cap every JOINTLY-silent run in both channels of `sc` to at most `max_silence` samples, keeping the audio and the shortened joint pauses in order and the stereo image
/// balanced. Segments BOTH channels of the whole clip `[0, len)` into frames of `frame_len` samples through ALES147, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is
/// `.silent` in BOTH channels, coalesces consecutive jointly-silent frames into runs, and for each run longer than `max_silence` cuts the excess (keeping the run's first
/// `min(count, max_silence)` samples) from BOTH channels through ALES2's `cut`, walking back to front so every earlier run's index stays valid. A frame silent in only one
/// channel is KEPT whole. Validates the whole span, band, floor, and frame length through ALES147's own checks BEFORE any cut; on refusal both channels are untouched.
/// `max_silence = 0` is exactly ALES148. An empty clip refuses `BadFrame`.
pub fn stereo_collapse_silence(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    frame_len: u32,
    t_low: i32,
    t_high: i32,
    silence_floor: u32,
    voice_split: u32,
    max_silence: u32,
) StereoCollapseError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the whole edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    const original_len: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // Segment BOTH channels once over one shared frame grid. ALES147 runs all of its (and ALES95's) validation here — band/span precheck, the floor ceiling, the
    // dividing frame length, the empty-clip BadFrame — read-only, before a single sample is cut. On refusal it leaves the source untouched, so both channels are too.
    var segs = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
    try stereo_segment.segment_stereo(sc, &segs, 0, original_len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, silence_floor, voice_split);

    // invariant: segmenting a non-empty clip yields at least one run per channel that tiles it — ALES147's own postcondition, relied on here.
    assert(segs.left.len >= 1 and segs.left.len <= segment.max_segments);
    assert(segs.right.len >= 1 and segs.right.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // The frames tile [0, original_len) exactly — the segmenter refused a non-dividing or zero frame length, so this division is exact and at least one.
    const frames: u32 = original_len / frame_len;
    // invariant: the frame count is real and never exceeds the mask that records it.
    assert(frames >= 1 and frames <= max_frames);

    // Forward pass — the SILENCE INTERSECTION (ALES148's own rule). Walk the frames left to right, advancing one cursor per channel across its own tiling run list, and
    // mark a frame jointly-silent ONLY where it is `.silent` in both channels. A frame silent in one channel alone stays unmarked — the other speaker's content keeps it.
    var joint_silent = [_]bool{false} ** max_frames;
    var li: u32 = 0;
    var ri: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        // Runs tile [0, original_len) in order and frame starts only increase, so each cursor advances monotonically to the run covering this frame's start.
        while (li + 1 < segs.left.len and segs.left.items[li].start + segs.left.items[li].count <= fs) : (li += 1) {}
        while (ri + 1 < segs.right.len and segs.right.items[ri].start + segs.right.items[ri].count <= fs) : (ri += 1) {}
        // invariant: each cursor names the run that actually covers fs — the runs tile the span, so exactly one always does.
        assert(segs.left.items[li].start <= fs and fs < segs.left.items[li].start + segs.left.items[li].count);
        assert(segs.right.items[ri].start <= fs and fs < segs.right.items[ri].start + segs.right.items[ri].count);
        if (segs.left.items[li].verdict == .silent and segs.right.items[ri].verdict == .silent) joint_silent[k] = true;
    }

    // Coalesce consecutive jointly-silent frames into runs and count the total excess over `max_silence` up front, so the length postcondition can be asserted exactly.
    // Each run's sample count is its frame span times `frame_len`; its excess is count minus the kept min(count, max_silence).
    var total_excess: u32 = 0;
    {
        var f: u32 = 0;
        while (f < frames) {
            if (!joint_silent[f]) {
                f += 1;
                continue;
            }
            const run_start_frame: u32 = f;
            while (f < frames and joint_silent[f]) : (f += 1) {}
            const count: u32 = (f - run_start_frame) * frame_len;
            if (count > max_silence) total_excess += count - max_silence;
        }
    }
    // invariant: the joint silence removed can never exceed the clip it came from.
    assert(total_excess <= original_len);

    // Backward pass — cut each over-long jointly-silent run's excess from BOTH channels in lockstep. Back-to-front is the whole trick: a cut shifts only samples after it,
    // so an earlier run keeps its original start index. Cutting the SAME span from both channels keeps them balanced at every step; the bounds are asserted, so neither
    // cut can fault and leave the pair half-capped. Walk frames from the end, closing each maximal joint run as it is entered from the right.
    {
        var b: u32 = frames;
        while (b > 0) {
            b -= 1;
            if (!joint_silent[b]) continue;
            // b is the LAST frame of a joint run; find the run's first frame by walking left through the marked frames.
            const run_end_frame: u32 = b; // inclusive
            var run_start_frame: u32 = b;
            while (run_start_frame > 0 and joint_silent[run_start_frame - 1]) run_start_frame -= 1;
            const count: u32 = (run_end_frame - run_start_frame + 1) * frame_len;
            if (count > max_silence) {
                const keep: u32 = max_silence; // min(count, max_silence), and here count > max_silence
                const excess: u32 = count - keep;
                const cut_at: u32 = run_start_frame * frame_len + keep; // keep the run's first `keep` samples, cut the rest
                // invariant: the excess lies wholly inside both current clips — it did before any cut, and only later runs (already cut) sat after it.
                assert(cut_at + excess <= sc.left.len and cut_at + excess <= sc.right.len);
                try timeline.cut(&sc.left, cut_at, excess);
                try timeline.cut(&sc.right, cut_at, excess);
            }
            // Skip the rest of this run — b will step down past run_start_frame on the next iterations, all already handled.
            b = run_start_frame;
        }
    }

    // invariant: both channels lost exactly the joint excess and leave balanced — the length holds identical on both, the original minus every excess-silent sample.
    assert(sc.left.len == original_len - total_excess);
    assert(sc.right.len == original_len - total_excess);
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE ZERO-CAP LAW — stereo_collapse_silence(max=0) equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both channels, across many frame lengths, floors,
//     and splits (the crux tie); THE CAP LAW — the result equals a by-hand keep where each jointly-silent run keeps min(count, max) samples and every other frame stays
//     whole, per channel, both channels equal length, a cap past the longest joint run unchanged, monotone in max; THE LOCKSTEP LAW — running mono collapse per channel
//     desyncs the pair where this holds them balanced; THE DEGENERATE / ATOMICITY LAW — an all-joint-silent master caps its one run, a no-joint-silent master unchanged,
//     a shared BadFrame / BadThreshold / BadFloor refuses before any cut, both channels untouched and balanced -----------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// The verdict of the frame that starts at sample `fs`, read from a mono segmentation by finding the run that covers it — an independent reference to the cursor walk
/// inside `stereo_collapse_silence`, so the cap law is proven against a second implementation.
fn frame_verdict(segs: *const segment.Segments, fs: u32) segment.Segment {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < segs.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (segs.items[i].start <= fs and fs < segs.items[i].start + segs.items[i].count) return segs.items[i];
    }
    unreachable; // the runs tile the span, so a frame start always lands in one
}

/// The hand-built expectation the stereo capper is checked against — segment BOTH channels of the ORIGINAL, mark each frame jointly-silent where it is silent in both,
/// coalesce into runs, then keep each jointly-silent run's first min(count, max_silence) samples and every other frame whole, on each channel. The capped pair must equal
/// this exactly, both channels the same length.
fn by_hand(out: *pan.StereoClip, src: *const pan.StereoClip, frame_len: u32, t_low: i32, t_high: i32, floor: u32, split: u32, max_silence: u32) !void {
    var ls = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.left, &ls, 0, src.left.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    var rs = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.right, &rs, 0, src.right.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    const left_samples = src.left.samples();
    const right_samples = src.right.samples();
    const frames: u32 = src.left.len / frame_len;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        const l_silent = frame_verdict(&ls, fs).verdict == .silent;
        const r_silent = frame_verdict(&rs, fs).verdict == .silent;
        if (l_silent and r_silent) {
            // Extend the jointly-silent run to its full frame span.
            const run_start_frame: u32 = k;
            while (k < frames) {
                const gs: u32 = k * frame_len;
                const gl = frame_verdict(&ls, gs).verdict == .silent;
                const gr = frame_verdict(&rs, gs).verdict == .silent;
                if (!(gl and gr)) break;
                k += 1;
            }
            const count: u32 = (k - run_start_frame) * frame_len;
            const keep: u32 = if (count < max_silence) count else max_silence;
            const rs0: u32 = run_start_frame * frame_len;
            try timeline.splice(&out.left, out.left.len, left_samples[rs0 .. rs0 + keep]);
            try timeline.splice(&out.right, out.right.len, right_samples[rs0 .. rs0 + keep]);
        } else {
            try timeline.splice(&out.left, out.left.len, left_samples[fs .. fs + frame_len]);
            try timeline.splice(&out.right, out.right.len, right_samples[fs .. fs + frame_len]);
            k += 1;
        }
    }
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const band_low: i32 = -1000;
    const band_high: i32 = 1000;

    // Six frames of four samples. The two channels carry DIFFERENT silences on purpose. Frames 1–3 are quiet on BOTH (one jointly-silent run of three frames = 12
    // samples), frame 4 is quiet on the LEFT yet loud on the RIGHT (kept — panned content the right carries), the ends loud on both.
    const left_sig = [_]i16{
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 0 — loud on both
        40,    -40,    40,     -40, // frame 1 — quiet on both (joint silent)
        30,    -30,    30,     -30, // frame 2 — quiet on both (joint silent)
        45,    -45,    45,     -45, // frame 3 — quiet on both (joint silent)
        35,    -35,    35,     -35, // frame 4 — LEFT quiet, right loud (KEPT)
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 5 — loud on both
    };
    const right_sig = [_]i16{
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 0 — loud
        41,    -41,    41,     -41, // frame 1 — quiet (joint silent)
        31,    -31,    31,     -31, // frame 2 — quiet (joint silent)
        46,    -46,    46,     -46, // frame 3 — quiet (joint silent)
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 4 — RIGHT loud (keeps the frame)
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 5 — loud
    };

    // 1. THE ZERO-CAP LAW — over many frame lengths, floors, and splits, stereo_collapse_silence(max=0) equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both
    //    channels — capping every joint-silent run to zero removes all joint silence, exactly the stereo stripper.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 24 }; // each divides 24
        const floors = [_]u32{ 100, 500, 1000, 5000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4 };
        var fli: u32 = 0;
        while (fli < flens.len) : (fli += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var capped = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&capped, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    var stripped = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&stripped, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    try stereo_collapse_silence(&capped, flens[fli], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si], 0);
                    try stereo_trim_silence.stereo_trim_silence(&stripped, flens[fli], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    if (!eq(capped.left.samples(), stripped.left.samples())) return error.ZeroCapLeftNotStripped;
                    if (!eq(capped.right.samples(), stripped.right.samples())) return error.ZeroCapRightNotStripped;
                    if (capped.left.len != capped.right.len) return error.ZeroCapUnbalanced;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 2. THE CAP LAW — over many caps, the stereo collapse equals the hand-built capped keep, each jointly-silent run keeping min(count, max) samples, both channels
    //    equal length.
    {
        const caps = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 12, 100 };
        var ci: u32 = 0;
        while (ci < caps.len) : (ci += 1) {
            var got = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&got, &left_sig, &right_sig);
            var src = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&src, &left_sig, &right_sig);
            var want = pan.StereoClip{};
            try stereo_collapse_silence(&got, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, caps[ci]);
            try by_hand(&want, &src, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, caps[ci]);
            if (!eq(got.left.samples(), want.left.samples())) return error.CapLeftNotHandKept;
            if (!eq(got.right.samples(), want.right.samples())) return error.CapRightNotHandKept;
            if (got.left.len != got.right.len) return error.CapUnbalanced;
        }
    }

    // 3. A KNOWN CAP — with frame 4, floor 500, max 2: the one jointly-silent run (frames 1–3, 12 samples) keeps its first 2 and cuts 10; frame 4 (quiet left, loud
    //    right) is KEPT on both. Result = 24 − 10 = 14 samples on each channel.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2);
        const want_left = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 0
            40,    -40, // 2 kept samples of the 12-sample joint run
            35,    -35,    35,     -35, // frame 4 — KEPT, quiet on the left
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 5
        };
        const want_right = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 0
            41,    -41, // 2 kept samples of the joint run
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 4 — the loud right that kept the frame
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // frame 5
        };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &want_left)) return error.KnownCapLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &want_right)) return error.KnownCapRightWrong;
        if (sc.left.len != 14 or sc.right.len != 14) return error.KnownCapLength;
    }

    // 4. A CAP PAST THE LONGEST JOINT RUN leaves the master unchanged — max 12 keeps all 12 samples of the one joint run, so nothing is cut.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 12);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &left_sig)) return error.IdentityCapLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &right_sig)) return error.IdentityCapRightChanged;
    }

    // 5. MONOTONE IN MAX — a larger cap keeps a master at least as long, both channels equal at every step.
    {
        const caps = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 12 };
        var prev: u32 = 0;
        var ci: u32 = 0;
        while (ci < caps.len) : (ci += 1) {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
            try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, caps[ci]);
            if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.MonotoneUnbalanced;
            if (sc.left.len < prev) return error.NotMonotone;
            prev = sc.left.len;
        }
    }

    // 6. THE LOCKSTEP LAW — the reason this rung exists. Running the proven mono collapse_silence per channel caps DIFFERENT runs: the left's joint region plus frame 4
    //    (four quiet frames = one 16-sample run) caps to 2, while the right's (three quiet frames = one 12-sample run) also caps to 2 — yet the left keeps 8 fewer
    //    samples, so the two channels desync. The stereo capper instead holds both equal by capping only the joint run.
    {
        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &left_sig);
        try collapse_silence.collapse_silence(&ml, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &right_sig);
        try collapse_silence.collapse_silence(&mr, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2);
        if (ml.len == mr.len) return error.MonoDidNotDesync; // the honest premise: per-channel mono collapse DOES desync here

        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.StereoTornApart;
        if (sc.left.len != 14) return error.StereoWrongLength; // one joint run of 12 capped to 2 → 24 − 10 = 14
    }

    // 7. THE DEGENERATE LAW, all-joint-silent — a master quiet on BOTH channels everywhere is one coalesced joint run: empty at max 0, unchanged past its length, capped
    //    to 2 at max 2.
    {
        const quiet_l = [_]i16{ 40, -40, 40, -40, 30, -30, 30, -30 }; // one joint run of 8
        const quiet_r = [_]i16{ 41, -41, 41, -41, 31, -31, 31, -31 };
        {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &quiet_l, &quiet_r);
            try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 0);
            if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.AllJointZeroNotEmpty;
        }
        {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &quiet_l, &quiet_r);
            try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 100);
            if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &quiet_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &quiet_r)) return error.AllJointIdentityChanged;
        }
        {
            var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
            try load_stereo(&sc, &quiet_l, &quiet_r);
            try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2);
            const wl = [_]i16{ 40, -40 };
            const wr = [_]i16{ 41, -41 };
            if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &wl) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &wr)) return error.AllJointCapWrong;
        }
    }

    // 8. THE DEGENERATE LAW, no-joint-silent — a master loud on at least one channel in every frame is unchanged on both. Here every frame is loud on the right, so
    //    NOTHING is jointly silent even though the left goes quiet in the middle.
    {
        const some_l = [_]i16{
            20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 40, -40, 40, -40, // loud then quiet on the left
        };
        const loud_r = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // loud throughout on the right
        };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &some_l, &loud_r);
        try stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 1, 0);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &some_l)) return error.NoJointLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &loud_r)) return error.NoJointRightChanged;
    }

    // 9a. AN EMPTY CLIP refuses BadFrame (nothing to cap), both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        if (stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2)) |_| return error.EmptySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongEmpty;
        if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyMutated;
    }

    // 9b. A RAGGED FRAME LENGTH — one that does not divide the clip — refuses BadFrame, both channels untouched.
    {
        const five_l = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        const five_r = [_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &five_l, &five_r);
        if (stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 500, 2, 2)) |_| return error.RaggedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongRagged;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &five_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &five_r)) return error.RaggedMutated;
    }

    // 9c. AN INVERTED BAND refuses BadThreshold (through ALES147's precheck), both channels untouched.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 2, 1000, -1000, 500, 2, 2)) |_| return error.InvertedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongInverted;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.InvertedMutated;
    }

    // 9d. AN ILLEGAL FLOOR — above the peak ceiling — refuses BadFloor, both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_collapse_silence(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 32769, 2, 2)) |_| return error.BadFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFloor) return error.WrongBadFloor;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.BadFloorMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadFloorUnbalanced;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-collapse-silence: the INTERIOR SILENCE-CAPPER CARRIED INTO STEREO, one joint silence capped in one lockstep — ALES100 caps each silent run " ++
        "of a mono clip to a maximum, keeping the pauses yet shortening the long ones (truncate silence to a quarter-second, without deleting the breath that makes " ++
        "speech legible). Carried into stereo it meets the wall ALES148 met: a StereoClip's defining invariant is that left and right hold the SAME length, yet ALES147 " ++
        "proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY, their silences differing in count and boundary. Capping each channel by its own silence would cap different runs " ++
        "to different lengths and desync the pair, so this rung decides ONE cap for BOTH. The rule is the JOINT-SILENCE CAP: read the jointly-silent frames through " ++
        "ALES148's silence intersection (a frame is dead air only where NEITHER speaker carries sound), coalesce consecutive jointly-silent frames into runs, and for " ++
        "each run longer than max_silence cut only the EXCESS (keep the run's first min(count, max_silence) samples) from both channels in lockstep, walking back to " ++
        "front so every earlier run's index stays valid. A frame silent in only one channel is KEPT whole — its other speaker carries sound; only the shared dead air " ++
        "is shortened, and only its excess is cut, so the breath stays. The crux is two laws. THE ZERO-CAP LAW: stereo_collapse_silence(max=0) equals ALES148's " ++
        "stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both channels — capping every joint-silent run to zero removes all joint silence, exactly the stereo stripper. THE CAP " ++
        "LAW: the result equals the hand-built keep where each jointly-silent run keeps its first min(count, max_silence) samples and every other frame stays whole, per " ++
        "channel, both channels equal length; a cap past the longest joint run leaves the master unchanged; monotone in max. Proven too: a known cap keeps exactly the " ++
        "named samples; the lockstep law (per-channel mono collapse desyncs where this holds both at 14); an all-joint-silent master caps its one coalesced run (empty " ++
        "at 0, unchanged past its length); a no-joint-silent master unchanged on both; an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each " ++
        "refuse by name — BadFrame, BadFrame, BadThreshold, BadFloor — both channels untouched and still balanced. It invents no new measurement and no new sample: it " ++
        "reads its silences through ALES148/ALES147 and removes whole excess-silent frames through ALES2's cut, on both channels in lockstep, fabricating no samples and " ++
        "reordering nothing; NOT a transcript or a real adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
